### Phase 1 characterization record

Repository evidence was collected at DwarfUICore HEAD
`d9e2e303848f4c1fceafb8be90a802a7ed18cc6d`. Sibling repositories were read
only. DwarfDirect HEAD `4616ceba` was clean; DwarfUI HEAD `33f5e525` contained
unrelated minecart-route work that remains untouched; DFHack HEAD `425442d`
supplied the native implementation evidence below.

#### Existing ownership and lifecycle map

- Provider acquisition runs from `dwarfuicore/services.lua` through the
  immutable provider proxy, the service-specific provider, `acquisition.lua`,
  `runtime.lua`, the service adapter, `api.lua`, and `identity.lua`. The process
  runtime owns one service record and facade cache per immutable numeric
  `ServiceKind`; `module_registry.lua`, the reload command, package inventory,
  and provider contract tests establish reload and distribution ownership.
- Context-menu input has one authoritative owner in
  `context_menu/input_hook.lua`. Its native trampoline wraps
  `plugins.overlay.feed_viewscreen_widgets`; its weak Lua-screen trampolines
  wrap `onInput`. Both dispatch before the predecessor, preserve exact
  arguments and packed returns, reconcile the current root, adopt compatible
  wrappers across reload, and leave foreign outer wrappers inert when retired.
  Handler failure currently disables the handler and delegates to the
  predecessor.
- `context_menu/service.lua` installs that one handler. `_MOUSE_R` sampling can
  call the sole `open()` transition. Selection, dismissal, registration
  removal, world-state cleanup, failure disablement, shutdown, and screen
  actions converge on `close(reason)` or its protected implementation. That
  close operation clears the session before presentation cleanup and is the
  required non-selecting close seam for prompt activation.
- Tooltip state remains authoritative in `tooltip/service.lua`.
  `tooltip/presenter.lua` reads each intent, resolves native-overlay versus
  exact current Lua-screen ownership, selects one transport in
  `tooltip/render_hook.lua`, lays out on revision or viewport-size changes, and
  asks `tooltip/renderer.lua` to paint after the predecessor completes.
  Renderer mutation invalidates the screen; pointer motion, clamping, resize,
  selected-owner dispatch, exact returns, foreign wrappers, and reload adoption
  are already characterized by the focused tooltip specs.
- Screen-pointer sampling uses `dfhack.screen.getMousePos()` in `pointer.lua`.
  Map sampling is demand-gated and calls `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()` in
  `pointer_poller.lua` and `context_menu/input_sample.lua`; nil is a normal
  unavailable-map result. Prompt completion must therefore sample once inside
  the `_MOUSE_L` release handler and must not reuse a polling or indicator
  sample.

#### Sibling and DFHack evidence

- DwarfDirect's fullscreen `DwarfDirectOverlay:onInput()` runs its interaction
  handler before competing dwarfmode behavior and returns true for handled
  targeting gestures, including mouse and Escape boundaries. Its fullscreen
  screen, command-specific validation, and private targeting state remain
  rejected because DwarfUICore already owns root-aware native and Lua-screen
  seams and the approved prompt accepts every sampled position, including nil.
- DwarfUI mood-popover list submission calls `open_row()`, whose default unit
  opener calls `dfhack.gui.revealInDwarfmodeMap(pos, true, true)`. DFHack's
  `Gui.cpp` implementation changes view/follow state before assigning
  `recenter_indicator_m`, establishing the native selection indicator while
  also proving why UserPrompt must assign the field directly instead of calling
  the reveal helper.
- `df.d_interface.xml` and generated `main_interface.h` type
  `recenter_indicator_m` as `df.coord`. The generated constructor initializes
  all three signed 16-bit fields to `-30000`; `df.coord:isValid()` uses a
  nonnegative x coordinate. The focused installed-runtime characterization
  snapshots and restores the field, writes a detached coordinate and the exact
  sentinel, and verifies `window_x`, `window_y`, `window_z`, `follow_unit`, and
  `follow_item` remain unchanged.
- Target source and installed `gui.lua` contain the same `paint_frame()` title
  implementation. For total frame width `W`, it paints
  `'  '..title..'  '` into `W - 2` top-border cells, starting at
  `x1 + max(0, floor((W - 4 - #title) / 2))`. A complete title therefore needs
  `W >= #title + 6`; narrower output is clipped deterministically from the
  right. Source SHA-256 is
  `1B42A70840C92A8AF15B912237FA78A6882406B993A40A2E341641058E9DD048`;
  the inspected installed function is text-identical even though its full-file
  SHA-256 is
  `0BB625D245B6593B773355F56BCEDFFD7B3898C525FBB2C4CCEE162FB7E8B4DB`.

#### Sufficiency and smallest-change verdict

- The input seam is insufficient as currently shaped. It exposes one
  context-menu-owned handler, has no prompt-first ordered consumer slot, and
  treats every handler failure as unowned by delegating. Contract major 1
  requires prompt-first arbitration and consumption after a prompt-owned
  failure. The smallest safe extension is to generalize this existing private
  owner into a deterministic two-consumer dispatcher: injected UserPrompt
  first, context-menu opening second, with pre-dispatch ownership
  classification and owner-specific failure notification. The existing
  native/screen trampolines, root reconciliation, predecessor contract,
  foreign-wrapper behavior, and reload adoption remain intact. No public input
  API, fullscreen screen, general registration framework, or parallel hook is
  authorized.
- The completed-render seam is sufficient. Its single selected owner can gain
  one private authoritative prompt-intent slot and one ordinary-tooltip
  suppression flag. The presenter can choose the prompt renderer while active
  and otherwise retain the current tooltip renderer and intent. The
  characterization proves presentation can be hidden while tooltip
  registration, selected intent, hook owner, and later resumption remain
  intact. No second render hook, tooltip registration, or public tooltip
  internals are authorized.
- Existing composite identity allocation is reusable, but map-registration
  handles and `api.lua`'s hard-coded map-handle validation are insufficient.
  Contract major 1 requires a distinct prompt-handle domain with service,
  contract-major, namespace, generation, stale/foreign/current classification,
  and terminal `cancel()`/`is_active()` behavior. The smallest change is a
  prompt-specific weak handle registry plus operation-aware private API
  validation; prompt handles must not masquerade as map registrations.

